\begin_layout Section
Algebra
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In mathematics,
 a binary operation is a king of binary function,
 i.e.
 given a pair of input values (operands),
 it produces an output value.
 Some definitions require the two inputs and the output to be elements of the same set.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A 
\emph on
binary operation
\emph default
 on a set 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is a function
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bullet_{X}:X\times X\to X,
\]

\end_inset

mapping pairs of elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 to elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Binary operations are usually written using infix notation such as 
\begin_inset Formula $a\bullet_{X}b$
\end_inset

 rather than by functional notation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 be a set equipped with one or several methods of producing elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 from other elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 (operations).
 A subset 
\begin_inset Formula $A\subseteq X$
\end_inset

 is said to be 
\emph on
closed
\emph default
 under these methods if an input of (purely) elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 always results in an element still in 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

,
 or
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\forall a,b\in X:a\bullet_{X}b\in X.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It follows that for a subset 
\begin_inset Formula $A\subseteq X$
\end_inset

,
 there is a smallest closed subset 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{A}\subseteq S$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $A\subseteq\bar{A}$
\end_inset

 (it is the intersection fo all closed subsets that contain 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

).
 Depending on the context,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{A}$
\end_inset

 is called the closure of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $A\subseteq X$
\end_inset

 is closed then 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{A}=A$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Sets with a single operation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A 
\emph on
magma
\emph default
 is set 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 with an operation
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bullet_{M}:M\times M\to M:x,y\mapsto x\bullet_{M}y
\]

\end_inset

that under which 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 is:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Description
closed 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall x,y\in M$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $x\bullet_{M}y\in M$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Minisec
Morphisms
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A 
\emph on
morphism
\emph default
 of magmas 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 is a function 
\begin_inset Formula $f:M\to N$
\end_inset

 that preserves the binary operation:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\forall x,y\in M$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $f(x\bullet_{M}y)=f(x)\bullet_{N}f(y)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
Special types of a morphism 
\begin_inset Formula $f:M\to N$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description

\emph on
monomorphism
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is injective,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description

\emph on
epimorphism
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is surjective,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
isomorphism 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is bijective.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When 
\begin_inset Formula $M=N$
\end_inset

 specifically:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description

\emph on
endomorphism
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is a morphism,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description

\emph on
automorphism
\emph default
an 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is an isomorphism.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A 
\emph on
semigroup
\emph default
 is a magma 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 the operation 
\begin_inset Formula $\bullet_{S}$
\end_inset

 of which is:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Description
associative 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a,b,c\in S$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $(a\bullet_{S}b)\bullet_{S}c=a\bullet_{S}b\bullet_{S}c=a\bullet_{S}(b\bullet_{S}c)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
More succinctly,
 a semigroup is an associative magma.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A 
\emph on
semigroup congruence
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\sim$
\end_inset

 is an equivalence relation on a semigroup 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 that is compatible with the semigroup operation 
\begin_inset Formula $\bullet_{S}$
\end_inset

.
 That is,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a,a^{\prime},b,b^{\prime}$
\end_inset

,
 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $a\sim b$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $a^{\prime}\sim b^{\prime}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $a\bullet_{S}a^{\prime}\sim b\bullet_{S}b^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 .
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Like any equivalence relation,
 a semigroup congruence 
\begin_inset Formula $\sim$
\end_inset

 induces congruence classes,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a\in S$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
[a]=\{x\in S|x\sim a\},
\]

\end_inset

and the semigroup operation 
\begin_inset Formula $\bullet_{S}$
\end_inset

 induces a binary operation 
\begin_inset Formula $\bullet_{\sim}$
\end_inset

 on the congruence classes.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a,b\in S$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
[a]\bullet_{\sim}[b]=[a\bullet_{S}b].
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Exercise
Because 
\begin_inset Formula $\sim$
\end_inset

 is a congruence,
 the set of all congruence classes of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sim$
\end_inset

 forms a semigroup with 
\begin_inset Formula $\bullet_{\sim}$
\end_inset

,
 called the 
\emph on
quotient semigroup
\emph default
,
 denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $S/\mathord{\sim}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
...
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A subset 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 of a semigroup 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

,
 is a 
\emph on
subsemigroup
\emph default
 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is closed under 
\begin_inset Formula $\bullet_{S}$
\end_inset

,
 or
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{A}=A$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A semigroup morphism is called a 
\emph on
homomorphism
\emph default
 specifically.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A 
\emph on
monoid
\emph default
 is a semigroup 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 the operation 
\begin_inset Formula $\bullet_{M}$
\end_inset

 of which (additionally) has
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Description
identity
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

element 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists!\mathds{1}_{M}\in M$
\end_inset

,
 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a\in S$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathds{1}_{M}\bullet_{M}a=a=a\bullet_{M}\mathds{1}_{M}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
In other words,
 a monoid is a semigroup with and identity element.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Exercise
The identity element of a monoid is unique.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathds{1}_{M},\mathds{1}^{\prime}_{M}\in M$
\end_inset

 be identity elements.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
...
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A 
\emph on
submonoid
\emph default
 of a monoid 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 is a subset 
\begin_inset Formula $N\subseteq M$
\end_inset

 that is closed under the monoid operation 
\begin_inset Formula $\bullet_{M}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathds{1}_{M}\in N$
\end_inset

.
 In this case,
 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 is a monoid under the binary operation 
\begin_inset Formula $\bullet_{M}$
\end_inset

 inherited from 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A 
\emph on
monoid homomorphism
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $f:M\to N$
\end_inset

 is a semigroup homomorphism for which
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f(\mathds{1}_{M})=\mathds{1}_{N}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A 
\emph on
group
\emph default
 is a monoid 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 the operation 
\begin_inset Formula $\bullet_{G}$
\end_inset

 of which (additionally) supports
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Description
inverse
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

elements 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a\in G$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists!a^{\prime}\in G$
\end_inset

 such that,
 
\begin_inset Formula $a\bullet_{G}a^{\prime}=\mathds{1}_{G}=a^{\prime}\bullet_{G}a$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Exercise
The inverse of an element of a group is unique.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a\in G$
\end_inset

,
 let 
\begin_inset Formula $a^{\prime},a^{\prime\prime}\in G$
\end_inset

 be inverse elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
...
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The (unique) inverse element of 
\begin_inset Formula $a\in G$
\end_inset

 is commonly denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $\neg_{G}a$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A subgroup of a group 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 is a subset 
\begin_inset Formula $H\subseteq G$
\end_inset

 that is closed under the group operation 
\begin_inset Formula $\bullet_{G}$
\end_inset

 and under the inverse,
 meaning that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a\in H$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\neg_{G}a\in H$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Exercise
A subset 
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

 of a group 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 is a subgroup of 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a,b\in H$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $a\bullet_{G}\neg_{G}b\in H$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
We have to prove that both directions hold:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a,b\in H$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $a\bullet_{G}b\in H$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\neg b\in H$
\end_inset

,
 therefore (because 
\begin_inset Formula $a,b^{-1}\in H$
\end_inset

) 
\begin_inset Formula $a\bullet_{G}\neg_{G}b\in H$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Conversly,
 ...
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Definition
A group
\emph on
 homomorphism
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi:G\to H$
\end_inset

 is a semigroup homomorphism.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Exercise
For a group homomorphism 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi:G\to H$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi(\mathds{1}_{G})=\mathds{1}_{H}$
\end_inset

,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a\in G$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi(\neg_{G}a)=\neg_{H}\varphi(a).$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
...
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\emph on
identity homomorphism
\emph default
 of a group 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 is the automorphism 
\begin_inset Formula $\id_{G}:G\to G$
\end_inset

 that maps each element of 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 to itself,
 i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a\in G$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\id_{G}a=a$
\end_inset

.
 An 
\emph on
inverse homomorphism
\emph default
 of a monomorphism 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi:G\to H$
\end_inset

 is a monomorphism 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi^{-1}:H\to G$
\end_inset

 such that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\varphi^{-1}\circ\varphi:G\to H\to G=\id_{G}\text{ and }\varphi\circ\varphi^{-1}:H\to G\to H=\id_{H}.
\]

\end_inset

Therefore an isomorphism (bijective homomorphism) is a homomorphism that has an inverse homomporhpism (isomorphism).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
The set 
\begin_inset Formula $\varPhi_{G}$
\end_inset

 of all automorphisms (homomorphism with an inverse) on a group 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

,
 form a group under the operation of function composition 
\begin_inset Formula $\circ:\varPhi_{G}\times\varPhi_{G}\to\varPhi_{G}$
\end_inset

,
 with identity element the identity homomorphism 
\begin_inset Formula $\id_{G}$
\end_inset

 and their inverse homomorphism as their inverse element.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Sequentially:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\varPhi_{G}$
\end_inset

 with composition 
\begin_inset Formula $\circ$
\end_inset

 is a magma.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\varphi,\vartheta:G\to G$
\end_inset

 automorphisms,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi\circ\vartheta:G\to G$
\end_inset

 is a bijective homomorphism,
 ergo an automorphism,
 therefore 
\begin_inset Formula $\varPhi_{G}$
\end_inset

 is closed under 
\begin_inset Formula $\circ$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\varPhi_{G}$
\end_inset

 with composition 
\begin_inset Formula $\circ$
\end_inset

 is a semigroup.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\varphi,\vartheta,\varsigma:G\to G$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $(\varphi\circ\vartheta)\circ\varsigma:G\to G:a\mapsto(\varphi\circ\vartheta)(\varsigma(a))=\varphi(\vartheta(\varsigma(a)))$
\end_inset

 because 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi\circ\vartheta:G\to G:a=\varphi(\vartheta(a))$
\end_inset

,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi\circ(\vartheta\circ\varsigma):G\to G:a\mapsto\varphi((\vartheta\circ\varsigma)(a))=\varphi(\vartheta(\varsigma(a)))$
\end_inset

 because 
\begin_inset Formula $\vartheta\circ\varsigma:G\to G:a\mapsto\vartheta(\varsigma(a))$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\varPhi_{G}$
\end_inset

 with composition 
\begin_inset Formula $\circ$
\end_inset

 is a monoid,
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\id_{G}$
\end_inset

 the identity element.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\varphi:G\to G$
\end_inset

,
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi\circ\id_{G}:G\to G:a=\varphi(\id_{G}a)=\varphi(a)$
\end_inset

 therefore 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi\circ\id_{G}=\varphi$
\end_inset

,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\id_{G}\circ\varphi:G\to G:a=\id_{G}\varphi(a)=\varphi(a)$
\end_inset

 therefore 
\begin_inset Formula $\id_{G}\circ\varphi=\varphi$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\varPhi_{G}$
\end_inset

 with composition 
\begin_inset Formula $\circ$
\end_inset

 is a group,
 with the inverse homomorphism 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi^{-1}$
\end_inset

 as the inverse element 
\begin_inset Formula $\neg\varphi$
\end_inset

 for each homomoorphism 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi:G\to G$
\end_inset

.
 By definition of the inverse homomorphism 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi^{-1}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\neg\varphi\circ\phi=\varphi^{-1}\circ\varphi=\id_{G}=\varphi\circ\varphi^{-1}=\varphi\circ\neg\phi
\]

\end_inset

as automorphisms by definition (codomain matches domain) point to the same identity homomorphism when composed with their inverse.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
